  faqHeading: "WordPress vs a custom website: what UAE buyers ask us",
  faqs: [
    { question: "Is WordPress good enough for a serious UAE business website?", answer: "Yes, for most of them. WordPress runs a large share of the web including substantial companies, and at under about a hundred pages with a short plugin list, a lean theme and real caching it performs well from a Gulf edge. What makes WordPress sites bad is neglect and plugin sprawl, not the platform. Budget for maintenance and it is a sound choice." },
    { question: "Is a custom website faster than WordPress?", answer: "A generated static site is faster by default because there is no code to run on request, only files served from an edge. A well-built and well-cached WordPress site can be fast enough that visitors cannot tell. The gap widens as you add a second language, a heavy media library and hundreds of pages, which is exactly what UAE bilingual sites tend to add." },
    { question: "Is a custom website more secure than WordPress?", answer: "A hand-built static site is, because there is no database and no code executing per request; the remaining risk is your deployment credentials. A custom application is not automatically safer, and can be worse, because its login and permission logic was written by one developer and reviewed by nobody. Small and boring is the security property, not bespoke." },
    { question: "How many plugins is too many on a WordPress site?", answer: "If you cannot recite the list from memory, it is too long. Under ten is defensible, twenty is a maintenance commitment somebody must fund monthly, and forty is a liability. Judge each plugin on its maintenance record rather than its feature list, and remember that migrating off WordPress later costs roughly in proportion to the count." },
    { question: "Will WordPress handle an Arabic and English site properly?", answer: "It will, with a multilingual plugin and a theme whose right-to-left stylesheet actually works. Test that on a staging install before buying the theme, because purchased themes often mirror nearly everything and the exceptions are what Arabic readers notice. Expect per-language caching and heavier fonts; subset and self-host the Arabic face rather than loading a full family." },
    { question: "Does a custom website cost more than WordPress?", answer: `Not necessarily on the build. With us, a WordPress business site and a hand-built static site both start from ${P.site}, and a large content site from ${P.seoSite}. The difference is recurring: licences, hosting that must run code, and a patching commitment on one side; almost none of that on the other, at the price of needing a developer for edits.` },
    { question: "What does a WordPress site really cost over three years?", answer: "Build, plus theme and plugin licence renewals, plus thirty-six months of hosting at your provider's current rate, plus someone applying updates, plus internal staff hours for publishing and repairs, plus an incident allowance sized by your plugin count, plus a year-three refresh. Hosting bands move yearly, so take the figures from your provider rather than from any proposal." },
    { question: "Can I move from WordPress to a custom site later without losing search traffic?", answer: "Yes, if the redirect map is built before launch rather than after the traffic drops. Content extracts cleanly from WordPress, so the expensive part is rebuilding whatever was implemented as a plugin: forms, calculators, members areas, multilingual relationships. Budget in proportion to plugin count, and never accept a migration quote with redirects left out." },
    { question: "Can I move from a custom site to WordPress later?", answer: "Usually yes, and how easily was decided when it was built. If content lives in structured files it imports readily. If it was embedded in code or a bespoke undocumented schema, extraction is a transcription job. Ask any custom vendor in writing, before commissioning, how the content comes out if you leave." },
    { question: "Should I use a page builder like Elementor or a custom theme?", answer: "A lean custom block theme if speed and staff discipline matter, since builders ship more code to the phone and add a second vendor's update cadence on top of WordPress. A builder if non-technical staff must restructure pages themselves and you accept the weight. Either way, a builder is a configuration, not a custom design, and should not be sold as one." },
    { question: "Is headless WordPress worth it for a UAE company?", answer: "Only when a real content operation and a real performance requirement both exist. It is genuinely good when earned, and it is the arrangement with the smallest successor pool in the local hiring market, which is a durability cost worth pricing. If either requirement is theoretical, you have two vendors and two failure modes solving nothing." },
    { question: "My WordPress site is slow and insecure. Should I rebuild it?", answer: "Often no. Cut the plugin list, replace a heavy builder with a lean theme, add full-page caching, subset the Arabic fonts, fix the media pipeline, and set up staging and backups. That is usually a fraction of a rebuild, improves what visitors actually experience, and gives you the maintained site you were missing rather than a different one." },
    { question: "Would you tell me to hire someone else?", answer: "Yes, and we do. For a straightforward WordPress brochure site with a monthly retainer, a developer resident in the UAE who can meet you, holds a local licence and shares your working week is often the better buy. BtechWaleTech is three freelance developers in India with no UAE office, licence, phone number or staff, and we say that before you ask." },
    { question: "When are you the right choice for this?", answer: `When the work benefits from being built rather than assembled: a large structured content site where volume and internal linking are the strategy, a bilingual site where Arabic performance is a commercial requirement, or an application where people log in and the site does work rather than describing it. Applications are quoted as software from ${P.software}.` },
  ],
